= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $e_1,e_2,e_3$ be the <basis> of the <defining representation> and $y_1,y_2,y_3$ its dual. On a diagonal element of the <Cartan subalgebra> their <weights> are $L_i$ and $-L_i$, with $L_1+L_2+L_3=0$. Use the <tensor product> and <symmetric square> <basis>
$$
T_{i;jk}=e_i\otimes y_jy_k,\qquad 1\leq i\leq3,\quad1\leq j\leq k\leq3.
$$
Its <weight> is $L_i-L_j-L_k$. For the three repeated <weights> put
$$
A_j=T_{j;jj},\qquad B_{i;j}=e_i\otimes y_i y_j\quad(i\ne j).
$$
If $\{i,l\}=\{1,2,3\}\setminus\{j\}$, the complete <weight-space decomposition> is
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{weight}&\text{basis}&\text{multiplicity}\\ \hline
L_i-2L_j\ (i\ne j)&T_{i;jj}&1\\
2L_i&T_{i;jk},\quad\{i,j,k\}=\{1,2,3\},\ j<k&1\\
-L_j&A_j,\ B_{i;j},\ B_{l;j}&3
\end{array}
$$
There are six spaces of the first type, three of the second, and three of the third. Their <dimensions> sum to $6+3+9=18$. The <weight diagram> below labels every space with all of its <basis vectors>.

\Image[paper-2-sl3-tensor-weights.png]
{title=All weight spaces of the sl3 tensor module, with each tensor-basis vector shown at its weight}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <contraction splitting of a defining module tensor a dual symmetric square>. For a quadratic <polynomial> $p$ in the dual variables, define
$$
C(e_i\otimes p)=\partial_{y_i}p,\qquad
\iota(y_j)=Q_j:=\sum_{i=1}^3e_i\otimes y_i y_j.
$$
Both maps are <intertwiners>. The first is the natural <tensor contraction>; the second uses the <invariant tensor> $\sum_i e_i\otimes y_i$. Directly, $C(Q_j)=4y_j$, so $C\iota=4I$ and
$$
W=\ker C\oplus\operatorname{im}\iota,\qquad
\operatorname{im}\iota\cong V^*,\qquad \dim\ker C=18-3=15.
$$
The vector $T_{1;33}=e_1\otimes y_3^2$ lies in $\ker C$ and is killed by both positive simple-root operators $E_{12},E_{23}$. It is a <highest-weight vector> of <weight> $L_1-2L_3=\omega_1+2\omega_2$. By <Weyl complete reducibility theorem>, it generates the corresponding irreducible summand. The <Weyl dimension formula> for $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ gives $\dim\Gamma_{a,b}=(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)/2$, hence $\dim\Gamma_{1,2}=15$, exhausting the <kernel>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{V\otimes\operatorname{Sym}^2V^*\cong\Gamma_{1,2}\oplus\Gamma_{0,1},
\qquad \Gamma_{0,1}=V^*.}
$$
The <kernel> has the nine outer <weights> of multiplicity one and each inner <weight> $-L_j$ of multiplicity two; the dual summand has just the three <weights> $-L_j$, each once.

\Image[paper-2-sl3-submodule-weights.png]
{title=Weight diagrams and basis labels of the fifteen-dimensional kernel and the dual summand}

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <basis> labels in the preceding diagrams. For each $j$, write $\{i,l\}=\{1,2,3\}\setminus\{j\}$ with $i<l$, and define
$$
K_{j,1}=A_j-2B_{i;j},\qquad K_{j,2}=A_j-2B_{l;j}.
$$
The contraction satisfies $C(A_j)=2y_j$ and $C(B_{i;j})=y_j$, so both $K$ vectors lie in $\ker C$. They are independent and form a <basis> of its <weight space> at $-L_j$.

Thus the <bases> marked on the <weight diagram> of $\Gamma_{1,2}$ are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{weight}&\text{basis in }\ker C\\ \hline
L_i-2L_j\ (i\ne j)&T_{i;jj}\\
2L_i&T_{i;jk},\quad\{i,j,k\}=\{1,2,3\},\ j<k\\
-L_j&K_{j,1},K_{j,2}
\end{array}
$$
and the diagram of $\Gamma_{0,1}$ uses the <basis> $Q_j=A_j+B_{i;j}+B_{l;j}$ at $-L_j$. Each $Q_j$ is complementary to the two <kernel> vectors because its contraction is $4y_j\ne0$. All vectors shown are therefore genuine <bases>, not merely <weight> counts. The <highest-weight vectors> are
$$
\boxed{T_{1;33}\text{ for }\Gamma_{1,2},\qquad Q_3\text{ for }\Gamma_{0,1}.}
$$
The latter is highest because $\iota$ intertwines the <dual Lie algebra representation> and $y_3$ is its <highest-weight vector>.

