= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <adjoint representation>, the <root-space decomposition> gives the eight nonzero <weights>
$$
\pm2L_1,\quad\pm2L_2,\quad\pm L_1\pm L_2,
$$
each of multiplicity one, and <weight> zero of multiplicity two from the <Cartan subalgebra>. Thus its <weight diagram> is the <root> diagram with a double zero <weight>, and its total <dimension> is $8+2=10$.

\Image[paper-2-sp4-adjoint-weights.png]
{title=Adjoint sp4 weights: the eight roots and the zero weight of multiplicity two}

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In the <defining representation>, the diagonal element of the <Cartan subalgebra> acts on the four standard <basis vectors> with <eigenvalues> $r,s,-r,-s$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{wt}(V)=\{L_1,L_2,-L_1,-L_2\},\qquad\text{each with multiplicity one}.}
$$
Their <weight diagram> is the four-point cross below.

\Image[paper-2-sp4-defining-weights.png]
{title=The four defining-representation weights of sp4}

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <tensor-product weight diagram> is obtained by adding every ordered pair of defining <weights>. Each $\pm2L_i$ occurs once, while each of the four <weights> $\pm L_1\pm L_2$ occurs twice, from the two orders of the summands. Zero occurs four times, from the ordered pairs $(L_i,-L_i)$ and $(-L_i,L_i)$ for $i=1,2$. Hence
$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{weight}&\text{multiplicity}\\ \hline
\pm2L_1,\ \pm2L_2&1\\
\pm L_1\pm L_2&2\\
0&4
\end{array}
$$
The <dimensions> sum to $4+8+4=16$, as required for $V\otimes V$.

\Image[paper-2-sp4-tensor-weights.png]
{title=Tensor-square sp4 weights, including the double diagonal weights and four-dimensional zero space}

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The printed phrase “not reducible” is a misprint: the requested nonzero complementary submodules establish that this tensor square is reducible. The natural reason is that the flip $\tau(v\otimes w)=w\otimes v$ commutes with the <Lie algebra representation>. Its two <eigenspaces> are the <symmetric square> and the <exterior square>, giving
$$
\boxed{V\otimes V=\operatorname{Sym}^2V\oplus\Lambda^2V,\qquad
\dim\operatorname{Sym}^2V=10,\quad\dim\Lambda^2V=6.}
$$
In the <symmetric square>, the four <weights> $\pm2L_i$ and four <weights> $\pm L_1\pm L_2$ each occur once, while zero occurs twice. In the <exterior square>, the four <weights> $\pm L_1\pm L_2$ each occur once and zero occurs twice; the $\pm2L_i$ are absent because repeated wedge factors vanish. These are the two requested complementary <weight diagrams>.

\Image[paper-2-sp4-split-weights.png]
{title=The complementary symmetric and alternating tensor submodules of the defining sp4 representation}

More precisely, the <tensor-square decomposition of the defining symplectic representation> identifies $\operatorname{Sym}^2V$ with the irreducible adjoint module $L(2\omega_1)$. The identification is an <intertwiner> sending a <symmetric tensor> $vw$ to the element of the <symplectic Lie algebra> $x\mapsto\omega(v,x)w+\omega(w,x)v$. For the <exterior square>, <symplectic contraction of an exterior square> has a five-dimensional <kernel>, the <primitive exterior square>, and the <invariant tensor> $\Omega=e_1\wedge e_3+e_2\wedge e_4$ supplies a complementary line. Thus the full irreducible decomposition is
$$
\boxed{V\otimes V=L(2\omega_1)\oplus L(\omega_2)\oplus L(0),\qquad
16=10+5+1.}
$$
Here $\omega_1=L_1$ and $\omega_2=L_1+L_2$. The two zero <weights> in the <exterior square> split into one primitive zero <weight> and the invariant line.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The ambient <group ring of a weight lattice> is
$$
\mathbb Z[\Lambda_W]=\left\{\sum_{\lambda\in\Lambda_W}a_\lambda e(\lambda):
a_\lambda\in\mathbb Z,\text{ with finite support}\right\},
\qquad e(\lambda)e(\mu)=e(\lambda+\mu).
$$
Addition is coefficientwise, and multiplication uses addition in the <weight lattice>. For a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> $V=\bigoplus_\lambda V_\lambda$, its <formal character> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}V=\sum_\lambda(\dim V_\lambda)e(\lambda).}
$$
The <formal character> records the <dimensions> of all <weight spaces>. The ambient lattice ring should be distinguished from the <representation ring of a semisimple Lie algebra>, which identifies with its Weyl-invariant subring.

For the $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ module $\Gamma_{1,2}$, $L_1+L_2+L_3=0$ and the <highest weight> is $L_1-2L_3=\omega_1+2\omega_2$. The decomposition $V\otimes\operatorname{Sym}^2V^*=\Gamma_{1,2}\oplus V^*$ gives
$$
\operatorname{char}\Gamma_{1,2}
=\left(\sum_i e(L_i)\right)\left(\sum_{j\leq k}e(-L_j-L_k)\right)-\sum_i e(-L_i).
$$
Collecting equal <weights> yields
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}\Gamma_{1,2}
=\sum_{i\ne j}e(L_i-2L_j)+\sum_{i=1}^3e(2L_i)+2\sum_{i=1}^3e(-L_i).}
$$
The six first <weights> and three second <weights> have multiplicity one; each of the three <weights> $-L_i$ has multiplicity two. Their total is $6+3+6=15$, as required.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $v\in V_\lambda$ and $w\in W_\mu$, the <tensor product of Lie algebra representations> acts by
$$
H(v\otimes w)=Hv\otimes w+v\otimes Hw=(\lambda(H)+\mu(H))v\otimes w.
$$
Thus its <weight space> of <weight> $\nu$ is
$$
(V\otimes W)_\nu=\bigoplus_{\lambda+\mu=\nu}V_\lambda\otimes W_\mu,
\qquad
\dim(V\otimes W)_\nu=\sum_{\lambda+\mu=\nu}(\dim V_\lambda)(\dim W_\mu).
$$
Substituting this into the <formal character> and using $e(\lambda+\mu)=e(\lambda)e(\mu)$ proves
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}(V\otimes W)
=(\operatorname{char}V)(\operatorname{char}W).}
$$
Every sum is finite for the <Lie algebra representations> under consideration.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Weyl group> acts on the <weight lattice> by its <root reflections> and extends to ring automorphisms of the <group ring of a weight lattice>:
$$
w\left(\sum_\lambda a_\lambda e(\lambda)\right)=\sum_\lambda a_\lambda e(w\lambda),\qquad
s_\alpha\lambda=\lambda-\lambda(H_\alpha)\alpha.
$$
<Weights> and their multiplicities in a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> of a <semisimple Lie algebra> are permuted by this action, so its <formal character> is Weyl-invariant.

Choose a set $\Phi^+$ of <positive roots> and put
$$
\rho=\frac12\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}\alpha=\sum_i\omega_i,\qquad
A_\nu=\sum_{w\in W}\det(w)e(w\nu).
$$
Here $\omega_i$ are the <fundamental weights>, and $\det(w)=(-1)^{\ell(w)}$, where the <Coxeter length> $\ell(w)$ counts <positive roots> carried to negative ones. For a <dominant integral weight> $\lambda$, let $L(\lambda)$ denote the finite-dimensional <irreducible Lie algebra representation> of <highest weight> $\lambda$. The <Weyl character formula> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}L(\lambda)=\frac{A_{\lambda+\rho}}{A_\rho}
=\frac{\sum_{w\in W}\det(w)e(w(\lambda+\rho))}
{e(\rho)\prod_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}(1-e(-\alpha))}.}
$$
The second denominator expression is the <Weyl denominator formula>. Both alternants change sign under each <root reflection>; their quotient is invariant. Although written as a fraction, the theorem asserts that it is a finite element of $\mathbb Z[\Lambda_W]$ with the nonnegative coefficients given by <weight> multiplicities. The <highest-weight classification> specifies which $\lambda$ label the irreducibles, while the <root system> and choice of <positive roots> determine every other term.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Write $u=\sum_\lambda a_\lambda e(\lambda)$ with finite support. The <root reflection> is an involution, and $s_\alpha u=-u$ implies $a_{s_\alpha\lambda}=-a_\lambda$. Any fixed <weight> has coefficient zero. For each nonfixed orbit choose its representative $\lambda$ with
$$
m=\lambda(H_\alpha)>0,\qquad
s_\alpha\lambda=\lambda-m\alpha.
$$
Integrality of $m$ follows because $\lambda$ belongs to the <weight lattice>; positivity is ensured by our choice of orbit representative. Pairing the orbit coefficients gives
$$
u=\sum_{\text{chosen }\lambda}a_\lambda[e(\lambda)-e(\lambda-m\alpha)].
$$
For each pair, elementary finite telescoping shows
$$
e(\lambda)-e(\lambda-m\alpha)
=(1-e(\alpha))\left[-\sum_{r=1}^me(\lambda-r\alpha)\right].
$$
There are finitely many chosen orbits, and each inner sum is finite. This proves the <root-binomial divisibility of reflection anti-invariants>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{u}{1-e(\alpha)}
=-\sum_{\text{chosen }\lambda}a_\lambda\sum_{r=1}^{\lambda(H_\alpha)}
e(\lambda-r\alpha)\in\mathbb Z[\Lambda_W].}
$$

For precision, the geometric-series expression is interpreted coefficientwise. Its coefficient at a <weight> $\nu$ is $\sum_{n\geq0}a_{\nu-n\alpha}$, which is a finite sum because $u$ has finite support. On each coset of $\mathbb Z\alpha$, reflection pairs the coefficients with opposite signs, so their total is zero. These cumulative sums vanish both before the smallest occupied <weight> and after the largest occupied <weight>. Only finitely many cosets are occupied, hence the product of the formal series with $u$ has finite support and equals the boxed quotient.

The individual terms $e(n\alpha)u$ need not vanish for large $n$. For example, $u=e(\alpha)-e(-\alpha)$ is anti-invariant, and its quotient is $-1-e(-\alpha)$. Thus **“finite sum” means a finite result after coefficientwise cancellation**, not an actually truncated geometric series. No analytic convergence assumption is involved.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Work over $\mathbb C$ with finite-dimensional <Lie algebras> and <Lie algebra representation> spaces. The adjoint form of <Engel theorem> is
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak g\text{ is nilpotent }\Longleftrightarrow
\operatorname{ad}x\text{ is nilpotent for every }x\in\mathfrak g.}
$$
Here a <nilpotent Lie algebra> has terminating <lower central series>, $\gamma_1\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g$, $\gamma_{j+1}\mathfrak g=[\mathfrak g,\gamma_j\mathfrak g]$. We prove the stronger linear statement: if $V\ne0$, $L\subseteq\mathfrak{gl}(V)$ and every member of $L$ is a <nilpotent endomorphism>, then there is a nonzero $v\in V$ killed by all of $L$, and $L$ can be simultaneously represented by <strictly upper triangular matrices>.

For the <proof of Engel theorem by induction and normalizers>, first establish a useful nilpotence fact. If $x^m=0$, left and right multiplication by $x$ on $\operatorname{End}(V)$ commute. Expanding their difference gives
$$
(\operatorname{ad}x)^N(T)=\sum_{j=0}^N(-1)^j\binom Njx^{N-j}Tx^j.
$$
For $N=2m-1$, every summand contains a power of $x$ at least $m$, so $(\operatorname{ad}x)^{2m-1}=0$. Restrictions and induced quotient maps remain nilpotent.

Now prove the common-kernel statement, the <Engel lemma>, by induction on $\dim L$. The zero algebra is immediate. Choose a maximal proper <Lie subalgebra> $M$. Its action on $L/M$ by commutators consists of nilpotent maps by the preceding fact. Applying the lower-dimensional induction hypothesis to its image gives a nonzero coset $y+M$ with $[M,y]\subseteq M$. Hence
$$
M\subsetneq N_L(M),\qquad
N_L(M)=\{z\in L:[z,M]\subseteq M\}.
$$
The <normalizer of a Lie subalgebra> is a <Lie subalgebra> by the <Jacobi identity>. Maximality implies $N_L(M)=L$, so $M$ is an <ideal of a Lie algebra>. Moreover $L/M$ has no proper nonzero <Lie subalgebra>, and every one-dimensional subspace is a <Lie subalgebra>. Thus $\dim L/M=1$.

Induction also provides a nonzero common annihilator
$$
W=\{v\in V:mv=0\text{ for all }m\in M\}.
$$
It is stable under $L$: for $m\in M$, $x\in L$, $v\in W$,
$$
m(xv)=x(mv)+[m,x]v=0,
$$
because $[m,x]\in M$. Choose $x$ representing a <basis> of $L/M$. The restriction of the <nilpotent endomorphism> $x$ to the nonzero space $W$ has nonzero <kernel>. A vector in this <kernel> is killed by both $M$ and $x$, hence by all of $L$. This finishes the induction. When an action is not faithful, induction is applied to its image, whose <dimension> is no larger than $\dim M$; no faithfulness assumption is hidden.

Starting with the common-kernel line, apply the same statement to each successive quotient <Lie algebra representation> space. It constructs a <complete flag> of <invariant subspaces>
$$
0=V_0\subset V_1\subset\cdots\subset V_d=V,\qquad
\dim V_j=j,\qquad LV_j\subseteq V_{j-1}.
$$
An adapted <basis> represents every member of $L$ by a <strictly upper triangular matrix>. Every product of $d$ such matrices is zero, so every iterated commutator with $d$ entries is zero as well.

Finally, if every $\operatorname{ad}x$ on $\mathfrak g$ is nilpotent, apply the linear statement to the <adjoint representation>. With $d=\dim\mathfrak g$, every product of $d$ adjoint operators vanishes, so $\gamma_{d+1}\mathfrak g=0$. Conversely, if $\gamma_{c+1}\mathfrak g=0$, then $(\operatorname{ad}x)^cy=0$ for all $x,y$, proving the other implication. **This proves Engel's theorem and its simultaneous-triangularization form.** The hypothesis concerns every element of the algebra, not merely a chosen set of generators.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Killing form> is the <symmetric bilinear form>
$$
\boxed{B(A,C)=\operatorname{tr}_{\mathfrak g}(\operatorname{ad}A\,\operatorname{ad}C).}
$$
The facts needed here are its invariance, $B([A,C],D)=B(A,[C,D])$, and the <root space> identity $[H,X]=\alpha(H)X$. Invariance follows from $[\operatorname{ad}A,\operatorname{ad}C]=\operatorname{ad}[A,C]$ and cyclicity of the <trace>:
$$
\operatorname{tr}([\operatorname{ad}A,\operatorname{ad}C]\operatorname{ad}D)
=\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}A[\operatorname{ad}C,\operatorname{ad}D]).
$$
Taking $A=H$, $C=X$, $D=Y$ proves
$$
\boxed{B(H,[X,Y])=B([H,X],Y)=\alpha(H)B(X,Y).}
$$
No normalization of $X,Y$ is needed for this identity. Semisimplicity gives nondegeneracy of the <Killing form>, but that fact is not needed for this particular equality.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the standard <coroot> normalization $\alpha(H_\alpha)=2$. The symbols $H_\alpha$ are vectors in the <Cartan subalgebra>, not the root-space vectors $X_\alpha$. The <root-string theorem> gives $\beta(H_\alpha)\in\mathbb Z$, so every <root> takes real values on
$$
\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R}=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{H_\beta:\beta\in\Phi\}.
$$
The simple <coroots> form a <basis> of this real space.

In the <root-space decomposition>, $\operatorname{ad}H$ is zero on $\mathfrak h$ and <scalar multiplication> by $\beta(H)$ on the one-dimensional space $\mathfrak g_\beta$. Therefore, for $H,K\in\mathfrak h$,
$$
B(H,K)=\sum_{\beta\in\Phi}\beta(H)\beta(K).
$$
For $H,K\in\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R}$ every term is real, so the restricted form is real. Moreover,
$$
B(H,H)=\sum_{\beta\in\Phi}\beta(H)^2\geq0.
$$
If it is zero, all $\beta(H)$ vanish. Then $H$ commutes with every <root space> and with $\mathfrak h$, hence is central in $\mathfrak g$. The <center of a Lie algebra> vanishes for a <semisimple Lie algebra>, so $H=0$. Thus the <Euclidean subspace of a Cartan subalgebra> has
$$
\boxed{B|_{\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R}}\text{ real and positive definite}.}
$$
Equivalently, positivity follows because the <roots> span $\mathfrak h^*$.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $H_{12}=E_{11}-E_{22}=[E_{12},E_{21}]$. The six <roots> of $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ take values $2,-2,1,-1,-1,1$ on $H_{12}$, so the <root-space decomposition> gives
$$
B(H_{12},H_{12})=2^2+(-2)^2+1^2+(-1)^2+(-1)^2+1^2=12.
$$
Using the preceding invariant-form identity with $\alpha=L_1-L_2$,
$$
B(H_{12},[E_{12},E_{21}])=\alpha(H_{12})B(E_{12},E_{21})=2B(E_{12},E_{21}).
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{B(E_{12},E_{21})=6.}
$$
This agrees with the <Killing form of the special linear Lie algebra>, $B(A,C)=2n\operatorname{tr}(AC)$, at $n=3$.

= iv
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <weight lattice> is
$$
\boxed{\Lambda_W=\{\lambda\in\mathfrak h^*:\lambda(H_\alpha)\in\mathbb Z\text{ for every root }\alpha\}
=\bigoplus_{i=1}^r\mathbb Z\omega_i,}
$$
where the <fundamental weights> satisfy $\omega_i(H_{\alpha_j})=\delta_{ij}$. The <coroots> use the normalization fixed above.

The <Killing form> defines the complex-linear map
$$
\mathcal B:\mathfrak h\longrightarrow\mathfrak h^*,\qquad
\mathcal B(H)(K)=B(H,K).
$$
By <nondegeneracy of the Killing form on a Cartan subalgebra>, this is an isomorphism. For $H\in\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R}$, expand its image in the dual fundamental-weight <basis>:
$$
\mathcal B(H)=\sum_{i=1}^rB(H,H_{\alpha_i})\omega_i.
$$
The coefficients are real by the preceding positivity calculation, and the $\omega_i$ are in $\Lambda_W$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathcal B(\mathfrak h_{\mathbb R})\subseteq\mathbb R\Lambda_W.}
$$
In fact both spaces have real <dimension> $r$, so this inclusion is equality.

= v
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Evaluate the <Killing form> on the <root-space decomposition>. For arbitrary $K\in\mathfrak h$,
$$
\mathcal B(H_\alpha)(K)=B(H_\alpha,K)
=\sum_{\beta\in\Phi}\beta(H_\alpha)\beta(K).
$$
Thus the <Killing dual of a coroot> is the <root> expansion
$$
\boxed{\mathcal B(H_\alpha)=\sum_{\beta\in\Phi}\beta(H_\alpha)\beta
=2\sum_{\beta\in\Phi^+}\beta(H_\alpha)\beta.}
$$
The factor two in the positive-root expression occurs because the contributions of $\beta$ and $-\beta$ are equal.

For $\mathfrak{sl}_3$, let $a=L_1-L_2$, $b=L_2-L_3$; the <positive roots> are $a,b,a+b$. On $H_{12}$ their values are $2,-1,1$. Therefore
$$
\mathcal B(H_{12})=2[2a-b+(a+b)]=6a,
\qquad
\boxed{\mathcal B(H_{12})=6(L_1-L_2)=12\omega_1-6\omega_2.}
$$
This uses the unscaled <Killing form> $\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}A\operatorname{ad}C)$. Changing that normalization would change the scalar six.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Work with a finite-dimensional complex <semisimple Lie algebra>. The <Dynkin diagram> is obtained from its intrinsic <root system>, with several structure theorems entering the construction.

First choose a <Cartan subalgebra> $\mathfrak h$. The semisimple structure theory gives the <root-space decomposition>
$$
\mathfrak g=\mathfrak h\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in\Phi}\mathfrak g_\alpha,\qquad
\mathfrak g_\alpha=\{X:[H,X]=\alpha(H)X\text{ for all }H\in\mathfrak h\}.
$$
The nonzero <roots> span $\mathfrak h^*$, <root spaces> have <dimension> one, and every <root> has its negative. The rank is $r=\dim\mathfrak h$.

Next use the <Killing form>. Its nondegeneracy on $\mathfrak h$ and its positive-definite restriction to the real <coroot> span identify the real <root> span $E=\mathbb R\Phi$ with a Euclidean space. Explicitly, if $t_\lambda=\mathcal B^{-1}(\lambda)$, put $(\lambda,\mu)=B(t_\lambda,t_\mu)$. The <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root> supplies its normalized <coroot> $H_\alpha$ and the integral pairing
$$
\beta(H_\alpha)=\frac{2(\beta,\alpha)}{(\alpha,\alpha)}\in\mathbb Z.
$$
The <root-string theorem> makes the reflection $s_\alpha(\beta)=\beta-\beta(H_\alpha)\alpha$ permute $\Phi$. Together with the absence of multiples other than $\pm\alpha$, this shows that $\Phi$ is a finite <reduced root system> and a <crystallographic root system>. These properties come from <semisimple Lie algebra> theory, not from the diagram's definition.

Choose a vector in $E$ that is not perpendicular to any <root>. Define the <positive roots> by positive inner product with this vector. The <simple roots> are those <positive roots> that cannot be expressed as sums of two <positive roots>. Root-system theory shows that they form a <basis> $\Delta=\{\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_r\}$, and each <root> is an integer combination of them with coefficients all of one sign. Distinct <simple roots> have nonpositive inner product.

Use the standard <Cartan matrix> convention
$$
A_{ij}=\alpha_i(H_{\alpha_j})=\frac{2(\alpha_i,\alpha_j)}{(\alpha_j,\alpha_j)}.
$$
There is one diagram vertex for each <simple root>. For $i\ne j$, the integral off-diagonal entries are nonpositive and
$$
A_{ij}A_{ji}=4\cos^2\theta_{ij}\in\{0,1,2,3\}.
$$
Connect the two vertices with respectively zero, one, two or three edges. The corresponding angles are $90^\circ,120^\circ,135^\circ,150^\circ$. A multiple edge carries an arrow **toward the shorter <root>**. For joined vertices, the length ratio is determined by $A_{ij}/A_{ji}=(\alpha_i,\alpha_i)/(\alpha_j,\alpha_j)$; hence the diagram records both angle and relative length.

For example, $\mathfrak{sl}_3$ has <simple roots> $L_1-L_2$ and $L_2-L_3$, giving two equal-length vertices joined by one edge: type $A_2$. For $\mathfrak{sp}_4$, take $\alpha_1=L_1-L_2$ and $\alpha_2=2L_2$. Then
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-2&2\end{pmatrix},
$$
so the two vertices have a double edge directed toward $\alpha_1$, the shorter <root>: type $C_2$.

Finally, orthogonal irreducible components of the <root system> correspond to the simple <ideals of a Lie algebra> in the <semisimple Lie algebra>, so its diagram is the disjoint union of their connected diagrams. Conjugacy of <Cartan subalgebras> and the <Weyl group> action on choices of <positive roots> make the resulting diagram independent of these choices up to isomorphism. **Thus the vertices, edge multiplicities, arrows and connected components encode the simple-root geometry and the simple-ideal decomposition.**
